---
import Post from '../../layouts/Post.astro';

export const meta = {
  title: 'Teaching a RAG app to show its work',
  date: '2026-10-31',
  description: 'What building AtlasVet taught me about citations, retrieval, and knowing when not to answer.',
  kind: 'essay',
} as const;
---

<Post meta={meta}>
  <p>
    A vet is in the middle of an emergency. A dog is crashing, and she needs the epinephrine dose. She doesn't need a
    chatbot that sounds confident. She needs the right number, and she needs to know where it came from.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the whole idea behind <a href="https://atlas.vet">AtlasVet</a>, the thing I have been spending most of my
    days on at Airvet.
  </p>

  <h2>What AtlasVet is</h2>

  <p>
    AtlasVet is a clinical-evidence Q&amp;A app for veterinarians. You ask a clinical question, and you get an answer
    where every claim points back to a source you can open and read yourself.
  </p>

  <p>
    And the sources are not "the internet". They are the references vets already trust: the Merck (MSD) Veterinary
    Manual, AAHA guidelines, product labels and documentation from drug makers like Boehringer Ingelheim, Zoetis and
    Dechra, and open-access papers from JAVMA.
  </p>

  <p>
    Around the Q&amp;A we have added the things vets reach for every day. 29 clinical calculators, drug pages, an
    emergency drug sheet for crash-cart moments, and client handouts that a vet can generate and hand to a pet parent.
  </p>

  <h2>The rule: no quote, no claim</h2>

  <p>
    The scariest thing an AI can do in medicine is make something up and say it nicely. So we built AtlasVet around one
    rule. If the answer says something, it has to quote the exact passage that says it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The model doesn't just write prose. It returns structured output where every claim carries the excerpt it is based
    on and the source it came from. Then, before anything reaches the screen, a plain old deterministic validator checks
    that each excerpt actually exists in the cited source. Not "sort of means the same thing". Actually exists, give or
    take a little formatting.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the check fails, we don't show a weaker answer. We refuse. I would rather tell a vet "I couldn't find good
    evidence for that" than hand her a confident paragraph with a made-up citation at the bottom.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are fancier ways to check faithfulness. LLM judges, NLI models, vendor grounding APIs. We looked at all of
    them. For the first version, a check that runs in under a millisecond, costs nothing and never hallucinates itself
    was the right place to start.
  </p>

  <h2>When being strict backfires</h2>

  <p>
    Of course, the strict rule bit us. After we added the Merck manual, a batch of test questions started getting
    refused far more often than they should have.
  </p>

  <p>
    The model wasn't making things up. It was being helpful. It would take two sentences from different parts of the
    same page and glue them together with "..." into a single quote. Every piece was real, but the glued-together quote
    didn't exist anywhere, so the validator rejected it.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fix was not to loosen the rule. It was to understand the mistake. Now the validator splits a spliced quote into
    its fragments and checks each one on its own. If every fragment is grounded, the claim passes. Strict where it
    matters, forgiving where the model is just being clumsy.
  </p>

  <h2>Retrieval is where answers are won</h2>

  <p>
    Everyone talks about the model. Most of my time went into retrieval, the part that decides what the model gets to
    read in the first place.
  </p>

  <p>
    As the sources grew, we split them across more than one knowledge base and searched them in parallel. For drug
    questions we also pull in sibling documents from the same product family, so a question about one formulation
    doesn't miss the label that actually answers it.
  </p>

  <p>
    That gave us recall. It also gave us a mess. We were filtering results with one flat relevance score, and it turns
    out scores from different indexes are not comparable at all. One corpus scored consistently lower than the others,
    so our single cutoff quietly behaved like a filter that hid a whole source. Other times we would stuff a couple dozen
    chunks into the prompt and the model would cite two of them. The rest was noise.
  </p>

  <p>
    Adding a reranker fixed both. Search wide to find everything that might matter, then let a model built for ranking
    decide what actually does. Recall first, precision second. It sounds obvious written down. It wasn't obvious to me
    until I watched the traces.
  </p>

  <h2>Not everything should be an LLM</h2>

  <p>
    The calculators don't use a language model at all. A drug dose is math, and math should be boring and predictable.
    Every calculator shows its formula, the variables, and the references behind it, under a little "How it works"
    section. When the guidelines change, like the RECOVER 2024 update for CPR drugs, we change the numbers and say so.
  </p>

  <p>
    The AI is good at finding and summarizing evidence. It is not the thing I want doing arithmetic on a 4 kg cat.
  </p>

  <h2>What I keep coming back to</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>In a high-stakes domain, refusing is a feature. Make it cheap to say "I don't know".</li>
    <li>Make the model show its work in a shape you can check with plain code.</li>
    <li>When a strict check fails a lot, read the failures before you loosen the check.</li>
    <li>Retrieval quality beats prompt cleverness, almost every time.</li>
    <li>Use the model for language. Use code for math.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    I didn't expect to spend this much of my life reading about chocolate toxicity thresholds in dogs. But here we are.
    If you're a vet, go try it at <a href="https://atlas.vet">atlas.vet</a> and tell me where it's wrong. That is the
    most useful thing anyone can do for it.
  </p>
</Post>
